# Lecture scripts and study material. Each slide is (title, [bullets], narration).
# Narration is read aloud by the lecture video; bullets are what appears on screen.

COURSES = {}

COURSES['ei-foundations'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('Why emotional intelligence matters at work',
         ['A skill, not a personality type', 'It shapes how you handle pressure, feedback and conflict', 'Anyone can improve it with practice'],
         "Emotional intelligence is the ability to notice emotion, understand what it is telling you, and choose a useful response. People often mistake it for being nice, or for staying calm all the time. It is neither of those. It is a set of habits. Because it is a skill and not a personality type, anyone can get better at it. In this lecture we will cover what emotional intelligence is and is not, how to spot your own patterns, a simple routine for pressure, and how to read a room more accurately."),
        ('What it is, and what it is not',
         ['It is noticing, understanding and choosing', 'It is not suppressing feelings', 'It is not the same as being agreeable'],
         "Start with what it is not. It is not hiding what you feel, and it is not agreeing with everyone to keep the peace. Someone can be highly emotionally intelligent and still disagree firmly. What matters is that they notice what is going on inside them, understand why, and then decide how to act, instead of being driven by the first reaction. Think of it as adding a short gap between feeling something and doing something. That gap is where good judgement lives, and it is the gap we will work on throughout this course."),
        ('The four parts of emotional intelligence',
         ['Self-awareness: noticing your own emotions', 'Self-management: choosing your response', 'Social awareness: reading other people', 'Relationship skills: acting on what you read'],
         "A useful way to organise the skill is in four parts. Self-awareness is noticing your own emotions as they happen. Self-management is choosing how you respond rather than reacting. Social awareness is reading what other people are feeling, from their words and their body language. Relationship skills are using all of that to communicate, give feedback and resolve disagreement. The parts build on each other. If you cannot notice your own frustration, you will struggle to manage it, and you will probably miss the same feeling in the person across the table."),
        ('Mapping your triggers',
         ['Triggers follow patterns', 'Note the situation, the person and the time of day', 'Name the feeling in one word'],
         "Most reactive moments are not random. They follow a pattern. A certain kind of email, a certain person interrupting, the end of a long day. The first practical step is to map your triggers. For one week, whenever you feel pulled off balance, write down three things: what happened, who was involved, and the time of day. Then name the feeling in a single word, such as irritated, anxious or dismissed. Naming it matters, because a named feeling is easier to manage than a vague one. After a week, look for repeats."),
        ('The ninety second pause',
         ['Breathe', 'Name the feeling', 'Decide the next useful step'],
         "Once you know your triggers, you need something to do in the moment. The ninety second pause is a short, repeatable routine for the gap between a trigger and your response. First, breathe out slowly, longer than you breathe in. Second, name the feeling to yourself in one word. Third, decide the next useful step, which might be answering, asking a question, or saying that you will come back to it. It sounds almost too simple. That is the point. Under pressure you will not remember a complicated method, but you can remember three steps."),
        ('Reading the room',
         ['Notice who is speaking and who is quiet', 'Watch for changes in tone and pace', 'Check your read with a question'],
         "Reading the room means noticing the signals other people give off, and adjusting how you contribute so your point lands. In a meeting, notice who is speaking a lot and who has gone quiet. Notice when the pace or tone changes, or when someone looks away. These are signals, not proof. So check your read with a question, such as, it seems like there are some concerns, what are we missing. Asking is better than assuming. Over time, this habit makes you the person who senses a problem early, instead of the one surprised by it."),
        ('Putting it together on a normal Tuesday',
         ['Trigger, then pause, then choice', 'Practise on small moments first', 'Review once a week'],
         "Here is how the pieces fit on an ordinary day. A message arrives that annoys you. You notice the tightness, which is self-awareness. You take your ninety second pause, which is self-management. Before you reply, you think about what the sender might be dealing with, which is social awareness. Then you write a reply that gets the result you want, which is a relationship skill. Practise on small moments first, not the hardest conversation of the year. And once a week, spend ten minutes reviewing which triggers showed up and how you handled them."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Pick one trigger to work on this week', 'Use the pause three times', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, choose one trigger from your map and commit to using the pause the next three times it appears. Do not try to fix everything at once. Open the workbook for this course and complete the exercises, because writing things down makes them stick. Then take the final quiz, which checks the main ideas from every phase. When you finish the last lesson, you will receive your certificate and a short personal review of how you worked through the course. Thank you for listening, and good luck putting this into practice."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Emotional intelligence', 'The ability to notice emotion, understand what it is telling you and choose a useful response.'),
        ('Trigger', 'A situation, person or moment that reliably pulls you off balance.'),
        ('Self-awareness', 'Noticing your own emotions and reactions as they happen.'),
        ('Ninety second pause', 'A three-step routine for the gap between a trigger and your response: breathe, name the feeling, decide the next step.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'Which situation at work most often pulls you off balance, and what does that reaction look like from the outside?',
        'When did you last choose your response instead of reacting, and what made that possible?',
        'Who in your team is hardest to read, and what question could you ask to check your read?',
    ],
    exercise=('Seven-day trigger log', [
        'Each time you feel pulled off balance, write what happened, who was involved and the time of day.',
        'Name the feeling in a single word.',
        'Use the ninety second pause and note what you did next.',
        'At the end of the week, circle the pattern that appeared most often.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['first-time-manager'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('The shift from doing to leading',
         ['Your job is now other people\'s results', 'Your attention is the scarce resource', 'Let go of the right things first'],
         "Welcome to The First-Time Manager. The biggest change in your first year is not a new title, it is a new job. You used to be measured on your own work. Now you are measured on what your team achieves. That shift is mostly about where you spend your attention. Many new managers keep doing the work they were good at, because it feels safe, and then have no time left to lead. In this lecture we cover one to ones, delegation and feedback, which are the three habits that make the first year work."),
        ('What to let go of first',
         ['Tasks others can learn to do', 'The need to be the expert', 'Being available for everything'],
         "Letting go is hard because the tasks you drop are often the ones you do best. Start with work that someone else can learn, even if they will do it slower at first. Let go of needing to be the expert in the room, because your value now is helping others do well. And let go of being available for every question, since constant interruptions teach people to depend on you. A useful test is to ask whether a task needs you specifically, or just needs to get done. If it is the second, it is a candidate for handing over."),
        ('One to ones that matter',
         ['Weekly, short, and about them', 'Start with their agenda', 'Surface problems early'],
         "A good one to one is the most useful management tool you have. Keep it weekly and keep it regular, even when it is short. Make it about the other person, not a status update for you. Start by asking what they would like to talk about, then cover what is blocking them, what they are proud of, and what support they need. The aim is to surface problems early, while they are still small. If you cancel one to ones when you are busy, people learn that they come second, and they stop bringing you the early warnings."),
        ('A simple one to one structure',
         ['Check in as a person', 'Their topics, then yours', 'Agree actions and write them down'],
         "Here is a structure you can use straight away. Begin with a short check in, how are things, really. Then cover their topics first, followed by yours. Ask open questions such as, what would make this week easier. Before you finish, agree on actions, who will do what by when, and write them down so you can start the next meeting by reviewing them. Keep notes brief and keep them private. Over a few weeks the pattern builds trust, because people can see that what they say leads to something happening."),
        ('Delegation without disappearing',
         ['Match support to the person and the task', 'Share the outcome and the why', 'Agree check-ins before you hand over'],
         "Delegation does not mean throwing a task over the wall. Match the level of support to the person and the task. Someone new to a task needs more guidance than someone who has done it five times. Share the outcome you want and the reason it matters, not only the steps. And agree check-ins before you hand anything over, so that you stay informed without hovering. Delegating and disappearing leaves people stuck. Delegating and hovering takes the ownership back. The middle path is clear expectations, agreed checkpoints, and trust between them."),
        ('Feedback that lands',
         ['Describe the behaviour', 'Explain its impact', 'Say what you would like instead, then listen'],
         "Feedback works best when it is specific and timely. Describe the behaviour you saw, without judging the person. Explain the impact it had on the team or the result. Say what you would like to see instead. Then stop talking and listen, because the other person may know something you do not. Give positive feedback with the same care, since people repeat what they are told works. Do not save feedback for the annual review. A small comment given the same week is easier to hear and easier to act on than a large one given months later."),
        ('Common mistakes in the first year',
         ['Avoiding the hard conversation', 'Treating everyone the same way', 'Forgetting to ask for feedback yourself'],
         "Three mistakes appear again and again. The first is avoiding the hard conversation, hoping it will fix itself. It rarely does, and the problem usually grows. The second is treating everyone the same, when people need different levels of support and different ways of hearing feedback. The third is forgetting to ask for feedback yourself. Ask your team what you could do differently, and then actually do something with the answer. Being willing to hear it models the behaviour you want from them, and it shows you are still learning too."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Book your next three one to ones', 'Choose one task to delegate this week', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, put your next three one to ones in the diary now. Choose one task you will delegate this week, and write down the outcome, the reason, and the check in date. Complete the workbook for this course, which has templates for each habit. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson you will receive your certificate and a personal review of how you completed the course. The first year is demanding, so be patient with yourself, and keep practising these three habits."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('One to one', 'A regular private meeting between a manager and a team member, centred on the team member.'),
        ('Delegation', 'Handing over a task with clear expectations, support and agreed check-ins.'),
        ('Feedback', 'Specific information about behaviour and its impact, with a suggestion for what to do instead.'),
        ('Check-in', 'A planned point at which you review progress on delegated work.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'Which task are you still doing that someone else could learn, and what is stopping you handing it over?',
        'When did you last leave a one to one with an action that actually happened?',
        'What feedback have you been avoiding giving, and what would the useful version sound like?',
    ],
    exercise=('Delegation plan', [
        'Pick one task you could hand over this week.',
        'Write the outcome you want and why it matters.',
        'Decide the support the person needs and two check-in dates.',
        'Hand it over and review it at the first check-in.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['leading-through-change'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('Change from the team\'s point of view',
         ['Change is usually done to people', 'Uncertainty feels like a threat', 'Resistance is information'],
         "Welcome to Leading Through Change. When an organisation changes, leaders often see an opportunity, while the people affected see uncertainty. They wonder what happens to their role, their routine and their team. That difference in view explains most of the friction. In this lecture we will look at why people resist change, how to explain the reason for it in plain language, how to spot resistance early, and how to keep momentum going after the launch, which is where many programmes quietly fade."),
        ('Why people resist change',
         ['They expect to lose something', 'They do not understand the reason', 'They doubt it will be seen through'],
         "Resistance is usually information. People resist when they expect to lose something, such as status, familiar routines or relationships. They resist when they do not understand why the change is happening, so it feels arbitrary. And they resist when they have seen earlier changes started and abandoned, so they doubt this one will last. If you treat resistance as an attitude problem, you will argue. If you treat it as information, you will ask what the person expects to lose, and that is a far more useful conversation."),
        ('Talking about loss honestly',
         ['Name what will change', 'Name what will stay the same', 'Do not pretend there is no cost'],
         "Being honest about loss builds credibility. If a team will lose a familiar process, say so. If some roles will look different, say so. People can cope with bad news far better than with the sense that something is being hidden. Equally important is saying what will not change, because that gives people something stable to stand on. Avoid selling the change as purely positive. A balanced message, this will be harder for a while, here is why it is worth it, and here is how we will support you, is both truthful and reassuring."),
        ('The change story',
         ['Where we are', 'Where we are going and why', 'What will not change'],
         "A change story is a short, honest narrative that you can repeat. It has three parts. First, where we are now, including what is not working. Second, where we are going and why, in plain language a new starter would understand. Third, what will not change, such as your values or your commitment to people. Write it in a few sentences, test it on someone outside the project, and remove any jargon. A good test is whether a team member could retell it to a colleague after hearing it once. If they cannot, simplify it."),
        ('Spotting resistance early',
         ['Silence in meetings', 'Quiet workarounds', 'Questions that circle back to the old way'],
         "Resistance rarely announces itself. Look for silence in meetings where you expected questions. Look for quiet workarounds, where people keep using the old process alongside the new one. Notice questions that keep returning to how things used to work. When you see these signs, do not push harder. Ask openly, what is making this difficult, and listen without defending. Often the problem is practical, a missing tool, a gap in training, or an unclear role. Fixing a practical barrier early is much cheaper than overcoming a hardened position later."),
        ('Keeping momentum after launch',
         ['Make progress visible', 'Celebrate small wins', 'Build short regular check-ins'],
         "Most change efforts fade after the launch. Energy drops, attention moves on, and old habits return. To keep momentum, make progress visible. A simple board or a short update each week shows people that the change is real. Celebrate small wins, because they prove the direction is right. And build regular check-ins into the calendar where people can say what is working and what is not. Small rituals, repeated, do more than a big launch event. The goal is for the new way to become the normal way, and that takes repetition."),
        ('Your role as the leader',
         ['Be consistent in what you say and do', 'Stay visible during the hard weeks', 'Ask for feedback and act on it'],
         "People watch what leaders do more closely than what they say. If you announce a new way of working and then use the old one yourself, the message is lost. Be consistent. Stay visible during the difficult weeks, rather than disappearing once the plan is approved. And ask for feedback on how the change is landing, then visibly act on at least some of it. When people see that their input changes something, they move from watching the change to taking part in it. That shift is what turns compliance into commitment."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Write your change story in five sentences', 'List the likely losses and your response', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, write your change story in no more than five sentences and test it on one person. List the losses your team may feel and how you will respond to each. Complete the workbook for this course, which has a template for the change story and a resistance map. Then take the final quiz. When you finish the last lesson, you will receive your certificate and a personal review of your progress. Change is hard for everyone involved, and your steadiness as a leader makes a real difference."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Resistance', 'A response to change that signals an expected loss, a lack of understanding or a lack of trust.'),
        ('Change story', 'A short honest narrative covering where we are, where we are going and what will not change.'),
        ('Workaround', 'A way of doing something that quietly avoids the new process.'),
        ('Momentum', 'The sense that progress is real and continuing after the launch.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'Think of a change you went through. What did you expect to lose, and did anyone ask?',
        'What would your team say is the reason for the change you are leading, in their own words?',
        'Which small ritual could you add to keep the change visible every week?',
    ],
    exercise=('Resistance map', [
        'List the people or groups affected by your change.',
        'For each, write what they may expect to lose.',
        'Write one honest sentence you could say to each group.',
        'Choose one practical barrier to remove this week.',
    ]),
)

COURSES['team-trust-reset'] = dict(
    slides=[
        ('What trust looks like in practice',
         ['Trust shows up in small behaviours', 'People say what they really think', 'Mistakes are shared, not hidden'],
         "Welcome to Team Trust Reset. Trust can sound abstract, but in a team it shows up in small, visible behaviours. People say what they really think in meetings, not afterwards in the corridor. Mistakes are shared early, instead of hidden. People ask for help without worrying how it will look. When trust is low, all of these shrink, and the team slows down without anyone quite knowing why. This lecture covers how to recognise the signs, run a team charter session, work through tensions, and keep trust healthy with a monthly check."),
        ('Psychological safety',
         ['The belief that you can speak up safely', 'It is built by how leaders respond', 'It is not the same as being comfortable'],
         "Psychological safety is the shared belief that you can speak up, ask a question or admit a mistake without being embarrassed or punished. It is built mostly by how leaders respond. If someone raises a concern and gets a sharp reply, the whole team notices and stays quiet. If they get thanks, the team notices that too. Psychological safety is not the same as being comfortable. A safe team can still disagree strongly, because people trust that the disagreement is about the work, not about them."),
        ('Signs that trust has dropped',
         ['Meetings with little discussion', 'Decisions revisited after the meeting', 'Blame when something goes wrong'],
         "Look for the signs. Meetings where nobody disagrees and then people complain afterwards. Decisions that are agreed in the room and quietly reopened later. A habit of finding who is to blame when something goes wrong, rather than what can be learned. People checking and double checking each other's work. None of these mean the team is bad. They mean that people do not feel safe being open. Spotting the pattern early gives you a chance to address it before it becomes the way the team works."),
        ('Writing a team charter',
         ['Agree how you will work together', 'Keep it short and in your own words', 'Make it visible and review it'],
         "A team charter is a short agreement on how you will work together. It covers things like how you give feedback, how you make decisions, how quickly you reply, and what you do when you disagree. Run it as a session where everyone contributes, rather than something the manager writes alone. Keep the language simple and use the team's own words. Then make it visible, on a shared page or at the start of meetings, and review it every few months. A charter that lives in a drawer changes nothing."),
        ('Running the session',
         ['Start with what works well already', 'Collect ideas individually first', 'Agree three to five commitments'],
         "To run the charter session, start by asking what already works well, so the conversation begins from strength. Let people write their ideas individually before sharing, so that quieter members are not drowned out. Group the ideas into themes, then agree on three to five commitments that everyone can actually keep. More than that, and nobody remembers them. End by deciding how you will remind each other, and when you will review. The conversation matters as much as the document, because that is where people learn what others expect."),
        ('Working through team tensions',
         ['Name the tension without blaming', 'Separate the issue from the person', 'Agree one small change to try'],
         "Every team has tensions. The aim is not to remove them but to work through them. Name the tension in neutral terms, for example, we seem to have different views on how fast to move. Separate the issue from the person, so that it does not become personal. Ask each side what matters most to them. Then agree one small change to try for two weeks, and review it. Small experiments are easier to agree to than large demands, and they build the habit of resolving things instead of avoiding them."),
        ('The monthly team health check',
         ['Three questions: trust, clarity, energy', 'Anonymous scores and open discussion', 'One action each month'],
         "A monthly health check keeps trust from drifting. Ask three simple questions. How much do we trust each other to be open. How clear are we on our goals and roles. How is our energy. Collect scores anonymously, then discuss the results together. Do not try to fix everything. Choose one action for the month and write down who owns it. Over time, you will see the scores move, and you will have a record of what worked. Consistency matters more than sophistication, so keep it short and keep it regular."),
        ('Your next steps',
         ['Schedule your charter session', 'Draft your three health check questions', 'Complete the workbook and the final quiz'],
         "To finish, book a charter session with your team in the next two weeks. Draft the three questions for your first health check and decide how you will collect the answers. Complete the workbook for this course, which has a charter template and a health check form. Then take the final quiz. When you reach the end of the last lesson you will receive your certificate and a personal review. Trust is rebuilt mostly through small kept promises, so start with one you can keep this week."),
    ],
    glossary=[
        ('Psychological safety', 'The shared belief that you can speak up, ask or admit a mistake without being embarrassed or punished.'),
        ('Team charter', 'A short agreement on how the team will work together, made by the team and reviewed regularly.'),
        ('Team health check', 'A short regular survey of trust, clarity and energy, followed by one action.'),
        ('Tension', 'A difference in views or needs that, if unspoken, affects how the team works.'),
    ],
    reflect=[
        'When did someone in your team last disagree openly, and how was it received?',
        'Which team agreement is most often broken, and what would make it easier to keep?',
        'What is one small promise you could keep this week that your team would notice?',
    ],
    exercise=('Draft a team charter', [
        'List three behaviours you want more of in the team.',
        'List three you want less of.',
        'Turn them into three to five commitments in the team\'s own words.',
        'Agree where the charter will live and when you will review it.',
    ]),
)
